    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Somerville served as Morgan County's seat from 1818 until 1891, when Decatur took over.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Somerville%2C_Alabama"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Morgan County Courthouse, built in 1837, remains the oldest intact courthouse in Alabama.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://encyclopediaofalabama.org/article/somerville/"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Known as the 'River City,' Somerville began as a river crossing for settlers moving west of the Appalachians.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://encyclopediaofalabama.org/article/somerville/"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Morrow Cemetery south of Somerville and the Old Cotaco White House are listed on Alabama's historic registers.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
